# A summer holiday told in the past: I went, I ate, I saw, and how it was.
lesson language-holiday v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "My summer holiday"
  goal "Say what you did on holiday in [[language.name]], in the past, and how it was."

  look {
    scene 35x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        comic summer who=["girl", "boy", "girl"] says=["[[phrase.what-did-you-do-in-the-summer]]", "[[holiday.i-went-to-the-beach]] [[holiday.i-saw-dolphins]]", "[[holiday.it-was-great-fun]]"] moods=["happy", "excited", "happy"] with=["", "", ""] thinks=[0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", ""] labels=["1", "2", "3"]
      }
    }
    say """
      To tell what you did, the words change: [[holiday.i-go]], I go, becomes [[holiday.i-went]], I
      went. [[holiday.i-eat|cap]], I eat, becomes [[holiday.i-ate]], I ate, and [[holiday.i-see]], I
      see, becomes [[holiday.i-saw]], I saw. Leo says [[holiday.i-went-to-the-beach]] and
      [[holiday.i-saw-dolphins]] To say how it was, [[holiday.it-was-great-fun]], it was great fun.
      To ask, [[phrase.what-did-you-do-in-the-summer]], what did you do in the summer?
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.holiday-name k=4
    }
    show language.holiday-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.holiday-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.holiday-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.holiday-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.holiday-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.holiday-write k=3 hard-k=5
    show language.holiday-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.holiday-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.holiday-write k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.holiday-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.holiday-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[holiday.i-went|cap]], I went. [[holiday.i-ate|cap]], I ate. [[holiday.i-saw|cap]], I saw. [[holiday.it-was-great-fun]] It was great fun."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the past and the present taken for each other, I went and I go:
    each naming question offers the same sentence in the present, and another thing done in the
    past. The words for went, ate and saw are new words in [[language.name]] rather than a word with
    a past ending added, which is why they are learnt as words. The step up from the postcard of
    this grade, which was written in the present with one we saw, is a whole account in the past,
    written by the child and read by a grown-up with the notice list. From this grade the written
    words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation
    is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is
    stuck, look at holiday photographs and say one thing done in each, in the past. Off the page,
    write a diary of the next weekend in the language, in the past, on Monday. It meets England's
    key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "understand basic grammar
    appropriate to the language being studied, including (where relevant): feminine, masculine and
    neuter forms and the conjugation of high-frequency verbs"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign
    languages: "Can write about themselves and familiar and simple matters by using simple words and
    phrases and basic expressions for which they are sufficiently familiar with the sounds while
    refering to example sentences" (Japan's grade six book We Can! 2 has a unit on the summer
    holiday, recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can describe in simple terms aspects of his/her background,
    immediate environment and matters in areas of immediate need". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[holiday.i-visited-my-grandparents]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[holiday.i-went-to-the-beach]] and [[holiday.i-ate-paella]] and the words to write are [[holiday.i-saw]] and [[holiday.i-swam]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[holiday.it-was-very-hot]] and [[holiday.it-was-great-fun]] and the words to write are [[holiday.i-visited]], [[holiday.i-played]] and [[holiday.i-swam]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
